    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures harmful-response rates and evaluates multimodal safety-alignment methods, but it does not test the claim's fine-tuning-induced safety erosion, token-position KL concentration, opening-token bypasses, safety localization, or deepening methods. Its results concern susceptibility to modality combinations instead.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports multimodal harmful-response rates and safety-alignment results. These are relevant to multimodal safety vulnerability, but do not measure any listed claim version. The rows below record reported C-ASR results as findings about the claim's broader subject, not as tests of a listed version. The paper's claimed modality-switching vulnerabilities are not evidence about first-token concentration or the specified bypass mechanisms.",
    "test_reason": "The study is an omni-modal safety benchmark measuring conditional attack success and refusal rates across 24 input-modality variants for 11 models. It also tests inference-time and post-training safety alignment on Minicpm-o-2.6. It does not fine-tune on harmful or benign data to erode safety (V1/V2). It does not measure per-token KL (V3) or use prefilling, suffixes or decoding attacks (V4); modality switching is a different bypass. It does not localize safety in parameters (V5), explain shallowness (V6), or train to spread safety beyond early tokens (V7). The paper does not cite or discuss the original sources. 'discusses' is the closest relation label but overstates engagement; it neither tests nor uses the claim.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. Add that the authors find inference-time methods (ECSO, CoCA, ShiftDC) less effective than post-training on image-text safety data. Post-training suffers out-of-distribution failure across modality combinations, and omni-modal cases benefit least from matched training data.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is a multimodal safety benchmark and alignment evaluation, not a reproduction of the cited fine-tuning or token-position methods. It evaluates modality variants and safety-alignment datasets and methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported C-ASR differs across input modality paradigms; the authors describe safety degradation as modality complexity increases.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Safety scores fall from unimodal to dual-modal to omni-modal inputs in all 11 models (Table 8, Figure 4). 7 of 11 models are most vulnerable on audio-visual-text inputs (Table 9).",
        "proposed_claim": "Text-centred safety alignment in omni-modal LLMs generalizes poorly to complex audio-visual input combinations, raising conditional harmful-response rates.",
